398
00:42:03.060 --> 00:42:11.110
Serhan Dagtas: Keep sensitivities along different dimensions like religion, culture, race, gender, age, etc.

399
00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:14.300
Serhan Dagtas: In mind when asking questions.

400
00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:24.449
Serhan Dagtas: Obviously, certain questions like sexual orientation is not in general appropriate unless the whole idea of the

401
00:42:24.630 --> 00:42:34.340
Serhan Dagtas: The survey is about that. And that may, in some cases, may have certain legal and ethical requirements that come with it.

402
00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:41.110
Serhan Dagtas: But even in posing questions, about things like income.

403
00:42:43.380 --> 00:42:47.820
Serhan Dagtas: Ethnicity, race, etc. You want to…

404
00:42:48.300 --> 00:42:54.460
Serhan Dagtas: Either provide a range, so not to be very specific about the income level, for example.

405
00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:59.660
Serhan Dagtas: And, or, by providing explanation about the…

406
00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:07.439
Serhan Dagtas: How the data will be used and how the identities of the people, of course, being interviewed or surveyed.

407
00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:11.660
Serhan Dagtas: Will be will be protected.

408
00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:24.210
Serhan Dagtas: We talked about closed-ended and open-ended questions, how they can be combined.

409
00:43:24.430 --> 00:43:30.279
Serhan Dagtas: And and how they can be used in the right order with the right mix.

410
00:43:31.480 --> 00:43:32.380
Serhan Dagtas: And.

411
00:43:35.150 --> 00:43:38.819
Serhan Dagtas: This is an example for what is called an NPS question.

412
00:43:39.890 --> 00:43:46.740
Serhan Dagtas: Which is very common and very typical. How likely is it that you would recommend this company to a friend or colleague?

413
00:43:47.800 --> 00:43:52.530
Serhan Dagtas: So, this provides a numerical Scale.

414
00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:13.920
Serhan Dagtas: But that number can be very subjective and can depend on, you know, large number of factors, so supporting this numeric.

415
00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:15.880
Serhan Dagtas: This is not…

416
00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:25.260
Serhan Dagtas: This enumeration with coming up with an NPS measure, an American NPS measure is important and very valuable.

417
00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:30.060
Serhan Dagtas: But it's often valuable when it's supported with.

418
00:44:30.400 --> 00:44:40.010
Serhan Dagtas: Responses to open-ended questions like in what ways, for what reasons they do, what, in what aspects.

419
00:44:40.180 --> 00:44:42.769
Serhan Dagtas: They would recommend or not recommend.

420
00:44:43.070 --> 00:44:45.630
Serhan Dagtas: This particular brand or company.

421
00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:05.959
Serhan Dagtas: Multiple choice questions is used very commonly in surveys.

422
00:45:09.050 --> 00:45:11.419
Serhan Dagtas: But just providing multiple.

423
00:45:11.870 --> 00:45:19.149
Serhan Dagtas: Response choices by itself is not sufficient in building an effective question.

424
00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:20.890
Serhan Dagtas: It's part of a survey.

425
00:45:21.690 --> 00:45:23.120
Serhan Dagtas: And

426
00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:35.460
Serhan Dagtas: The multiple choice questions may allow multiple responses or may not allow them. You may need to choose or force the

427
00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:42.010
Serhan Dagtas: Respondent choose only one of the possible answers and need some questions.

428
00:45:47.720 --> 00:45:49.520
Serhan Dagtas: And if there are multiple.

429
00:45:49.640 --> 00:45:54.579
Serhan Dagtas: Options that are provided using this drop down.

430
00:45:55.450 --> 00:46:00.439
Serhan Dagtas: Answer technique may also be helpful.

431
00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:09.709
Serhan Dagtas: And this also provides a kind of a numeric ordering and or sorting of the responses so you can very easily find.

432
00:46:10.150 --> 00:46:20.369
Serhan Dagtas: As compared to the multiple choice responses, the kind of age range that you would respond with in this case.

433
00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:32.369
Serhan Dagtas: Multiple options as part of the answers to a question can also be used in a ranking situation, assorted.

434
00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:42.559
Serhan Dagtas: List of responses or the, Survey respondents will basically rank.

435
00:46:43.850 --> 00:46:49.810
Serhan Dagtas: These… Options, in this example, there are a bunch of shows.

436
00:46:49.940 --> 00:46:55.469
Serhan Dagtas: Ranking or sorting them from the favorite

437
00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:00.580
Serhan Dagtas: Go down to the least favorite one.

438
00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:16.249
Serhan Dagtas: Another common technique is matrix questions, a bunch of questions combined together.

439
00:47:16.460 --> 00:47:19.989
Serhan Dagtas: So that, responses to them.

440
00:47:20.700 --> 00:47:23.800
Serhan Dagtas: In in the horizontal dimension typically.

441
00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:26.299
Serhan Dagtas: Can be.

442
00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:28.140
Serhan Dagtas: Provided.

443
00:47:28.670 --> 00:47:29.820
Serhan Dagtas: The.

444
00:47:29.950 --> 00:47:39.030
Serhan Dagtas: For a set of questions rather than individual questions, which may, of course, be a time-saving process and space-saving method as well.

445
00:47:42.970 --> 00:47:48.499
Serhan Dagtas: But in some specific cases, like the lack of space.

446
00:47:48.710 --> 00:47:50.890
Serhan Dagtas: When people are accessing these.

447
00:47:51.340 --> 00:48:04.100
Serhan Dagtas: surveys through their mobile devices with small screens, for example, that may be a consideration to keep in mind. So don't assume that they're working with large screens, or obviously paper or pencil.

448
00:48:04.510 --> 00:48:07.760
Serhan Dagtas: And the response options should be brief.

449
00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:11.340
Serhan Dagtas: Not to overpopulate the matrix.

450
00:48:11.560 --> 00:48:17.620
Serhan Dagtas: And make it harder to read and respond for the participants.

451
00:48:18.290 --> 00:48:19.750
Serhan Dagtas: And

452
00:48:20.160 --> 00:48:28.440
Serhan Dagtas: Fewer response options are a common technique when there's a matrix or a list of multiple questions.

453
00:48:28.600 --> 00:48:30.949
Serhan Dagtas: That can be answered at the same time.

454
00:48:38.360 --> 00:48:45.500
Serhan Dagtas: When you use rating scales, rate your response, rate your satisfaction, rate your,

455
00:48:45.650 --> 00:48:51.109
Serhan Dagtas: Opinion et cetera the rating can be based on.

456
00:48:51.980 --> 00:48:53.620
Serhan Dagtas: Worded statements.

457
00:48:54.210 --> 00:48:58.859
Serhan Dagtas: Like, how easy was it to find what you were looking for?

458
00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:07.890
Serhan Dagtas: The ratings can be based on the wording, extremely easy, very easy, somewhat easy, etc. Or you can use numeric

459
00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:10.900
Serhan Dagtas: Ratings.

460
00:49:11.500 --> 00:49:13.000
Serhan Dagtas: Like this scale.

461
00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:25.239
Serhan Dagtas: Obviously, if you're doing any quantitative analysis at a later point, you can convert these worded ratings.

462
00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:28.539
Serhan Dagtas: Into numeric equivalents.

463
00:49:31.530 --> 00:49:36.159
Serhan Dagtas: But if that's the ultimate goal, it may be more accurate.

464
00:49:37.080 --> 00:49:40.550
Serhan Dagtas: To use, to do that with numbers.

465
00:49:40.900 --> 00:49:46.190
Serhan Dagtas: So the respondents themselves can choose the numeric.

466
00:49:46.830 --> 00:49:47.900
Serhan Dagtas: The.

467
00:49:48.170 --> 00:49:54.019
Serhan Dagtas: Meaning that they would assign to their responses as, as they respond to a question.

468
00:50:12.590 --> 00:50:22.280
Serhan Dagtas: And when the response, the number of responses is limited to only two, that obviously will make a very specific…

469
00:50:23.950 --> 00:50:35.999
Serhan Dagtas: A statement about the respondent's position. Do you feel you received the necessary support and guidance from your manager? So this obviously requires them to.

470
00:50:37.500 --> 00:50:38.950
Serhan Dagtas: Choose.

471
00:50:39.350 --> 00:50:45.879
Serhan Dagtas: point, rather than… Good, providing a gradual response.

472
00:50:46.350 --> 00:50:57.530
Serhan Dagtas: Or to rate or evaluate the level of support or guidance they support. So they're expected to respond with a binary.

473
00:50:58.100 --> 00:50:59.390
Serhan Dagtas: Evaluation.

474
00:50:59.690 --> 00:51:01.759
Serhan Dagtas: They give it a yes or no question.

475
00:51:03.130 --> 00:51:08.210
Serhan Dagtas: So the responses to this question should also be analyzed.

476
00:51:08.730 --> 00:51:12.139
Serhan Dagtas: And evaluated with this.

477
00:51:12.370 --> 00:51:15.749
Serhan Dagtas: Point in mind that this is a.

478
00:51:16.550 --> 00:51:21.640
Serhan Dagtas: Of a strong statement that the, the respondent is providing.

479
00:51:22.340 --> 00:51:30.980
Serhan Dagtas: as part of their response. More commonly, 5 or 7 point scales can be used as we saw in the examples.

480
00:51:31.280 --> 00:51:39.870
Serhan Dagtas: Providing 5 different levels, I'm satisfied with the culture of my workplace, so 5 different levels, going from strongly disagree to

481
00:51:40.610 --> 00:51:42.740
Serhan Dagtas: Strongly agree.

482
00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:49.720
Serhan Dagtas: Or you may provide more or less precision and higher number of levels.

483
00:51:49.930 --> 00:51:55.959
Serhan Dagtas: In terms of… Worded statements or in terms of numeric scales.

484
00:51:56.940 --> 00:51:59.180
Serhan Dagtas: Answers to these questions.

485
00:52:00.010 --> 00:52:02.340
Serhan Dagtas: How long should your survey be?

486
00:52:02.700 --> 00:52:11.779
Serhan Dagtas: You may have received these surveys written or maybe on the phone surveys a lot and obviously

487
00:52:11.940 --> 00:52:14.199
Serhan Dagtas: You get a higher response rate.

488
00:52:17.240 --> 00:52:21.539
Serhan Dagtas: Or shorter and more focused and more concise surveys.

489
00:52:24.110 --> 00:52:38.450
Serhan Dagtas: There's some research here that Indicates that 53… percent of surveys Contain 5 or fewer questions.

490
00:52:38.600 --> 00:52:52.840
Serhan Dagtas: Which may not be easy for all survey types, obviously, but the fewer questions, the better organized your survey, the higher the likelihood of getting a sufficient number of responses. That's always the case.

491
00:52:53.200 --> 00:53:00.240
Serhan Dagtas: And there's some tips about keeping surveys short and relevant, meaning the questions.

492
00:53:00.410 --> 00:53:03.990
Serhan Dagtas: Relevant to your, your goal in the first place.

493
00:53:05.240 --> 00:53:09.560
Serhan Dagtas: 10 or fewer questions is a good general advice.

494
00:53:10.440 --> 00:53:11.210
Serhan Dagtas: And…

495
00:53:16.150 --> 00:53:25.660
Serhan Dagtas: And you may want to qualify or disqualify some respondents if you want to really survey the users of a certain product.

496
00:53:25.920 --> 00:53:33.580
Serhan Dagtas: You don't want to include responses from non-users, for example, so the screening questions can help you narrow down.

497
00:53:33.860 --> 00:53:36.440
Serhan Dagtas: To a particular.

498
00:53:37.170 --> 00:53:38.020
Serhan Dagtas: group.

499
00:53:42.790 --> 00:53:46.650
Serhan Dagtas: And follow-up questions may also help keep your.

500
00:53:46.930 --> 00:53:49.150
Serhan Dagtas: Survey short and relevant.

501
00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:59.020
Serhan Dagtas: And, which can be done at a later point for the respondents of your survey.

502
00:54:04.620 --> 00:54:09.070
Serhan Dagtas: The order of the questions in your surveys can be as important.

503
00:54:09.450 --> 00:54:14.719
Serhan Dagtas: and critical as the content of the survey questions.

504
00:54:16.470 --> 00:54:17.280
Serhan Dagtas: Good.

505
00:54:22.180 --> 00:54:30.739
Serhan Dagtas: It's not always effective, but if the questions are not really related to each other, randomizing them may help reduce the

506
00:54:31.340 --> 00:54:38.039
Serhan Dagtas: The possibility of a bias that may be caused by a certain order of the questions.

507
00:54:38.650 --> 00:54:43.689
Serhan Dagtas: But, more commonly organizing them.

508
00:54:43.800 --> 00:54:53.700
Serhan Dagtas: by providing open but ended questions, for example, towards the end of the survey will be more helpful in most cases rather than using a random order.

509
00:55:04.170 --> 00:55:07.390
Serhan Dagtas: Any screening questions, of course, come, come first.

510
00:55:07.670 --> 00:55:14.480
Serhan Dagtas: And, and the sensitive questions should come at the end and we did mention a few of these key points.

511
00:55:15.070 --> 00:55:21.699
Serhan Dagtas: And many surveys today may use different types of incentives, products, promotions.

512
00:55:21.920 --> 00:55:25.190
Serhan Dagtas: Or even cash incentives.

513
00:55:25.400 --> 00:55:32.389
Serhan Dagtas: To encourage, higher levels of, of responses you may have received some surveys.

514
00:55:32.910 --> 00:55:34.069
Serhan Dagtas: In mail.

515
00:55:34.740 --> 00:55:44.989
Serhan Dagtas: And sometimes they will come with some kind of an incentive mechanism. I've seen myself even surveys being mailed to me with some

516
00:55:45.260 --> 00:55:49.509
Serhan Dagtas: Actual cash in them, typically a dollar bill.

517
00:55:49.710 --> 00:55:53.080
Serhan Dagtas: Which I find a very interesting and effective way.

